| [1.5ct D-VS2 (GIA)](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29242552?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | [$8,764](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29242552?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | [$9,624](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-by-james-allen-item-311235?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | D color — the top natural color grade. $1,060 more than G-VS2 for a colorless stone that reads marginally brighter. |
| [1.5ct G-VVS2 (GIA)](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27336596?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | [$8,879](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27336596?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | [$9,739](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-by-james-allen-item-311235?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | G color raised to VVS2 clarity. $1,175 more than G-VS2 for a clarity grade invisible without a loupe. |
| [1.5ct D-VS1 (GIA)](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28800770?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | [$9,024](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28800770?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | [$9,884](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-by-james-allen-item-311235?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | D-VS1 — top color, high clarity. $1,320 more than G-VS2. Both grades exceed what the naked eye can detect. |

**Verdict:** G-VS2 at [$8,564 total](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29651855?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the correct natural entry at 1.5ct. It sits at the same G-VS2 floor that applies at every weight — near-colorless, eye-clean, GIA certified. F-VS2 at $8,807 is worth considering for a white metal solitaire where you want color confidence without a side-by-side comparison. Above F-VS2, you are buying certificates, not visible improvement — and the stones cost $1,000+ more for grades that require a loupe to justify.

> "The G-VS2 floor does not change at 1.5ct — and I have seen buyers try to push it. They move to H-VS2 to save a few hundred on a stone that already costs $8,500. Do not do it. At 1.5ct, color shows more than it did at 1ct. Stay at G. An $8,564 ring at the correct grade will look better in ten years than a compromised stone at the same budget ever will." — **Farzana, Diamond Expert at DiamondCritics**

