## Frequently Asked Questions

### Is 1.5ct noticeably bigger than 1ct in a radiant cut?

Yes — but the difference is more visible in photos and side-by-side comparisons than in daily wear. A 1ct radiant measures approximately 6.3 × 5.3mm and a 1.5ct measures approximately 7.0 × 5.8mm — a 0.7mm length and 0.5mm width increase. That is genuine visible size, but it is not 50% more visual diamond even though it is 50% more carat weight.

### What is the 1.5ct Jump Rule?

The 1.5ct Jump Rule is the principle that the price jump from 1ct to 1.5ct is far steeper than the size jump. In natural diamonds, the stone price more than triples while face-up area grows by only ~12%. For lab diamonds, the stone price roughly doubles for the same ~12% face-up increase — which is why the jump makes far more financial sense in lab.

### What is the Weight-to-Width Gap?

The Weight-to-Width Gap is the disconnect between how much 1.5ct costs and how much larger it looks. Buyers often expect 50% more visual diamond because they are paying for 50% more carat weight — but the face-up size increase is closer to 10–12%. Understanding this gap means you buy 1.5ct for the right reason: because you want a larger stone, not because the price implies something the dimensions cannot deliver.

### What color grade should I buy in a 1.5ct radiant cut?

G is the correct floor for natural stones — near-colorless at this weight and the lowest grade that reads white in a standard prong setting. At 1.5ct, F becomes slightly more justifiable than at 1ct because the larger stone makes subtle body color marginally more visible. In yellow or rose gold, G is ideal with no upgrade needed — the warm metal absorbs any tint naturally.

### What clarity grade do I need at 1.5ct?

VS2 is the clarity floor. At 1.5ct, a VS2 radiant cut is reliably eye-clean — no inclusions visible to the naked eye.

Some SI1 stones pass eye-clean at this weight, but the radiant's center facet pattern can expose inclusions more readily than a round brilliant, so VS2 is the safer baseline unless you are reviewing the specific stone's grading plot. Above VS2, clarity improvements require a loupe to detect.

### Is lab or natural better at 1.5ct?

Lab is the stronger value at 1.5ct — D-VVS1 for $1,772 versus G-VS2 natural for $7,704, for stones that are chemically identical, visually indistinguishable, and equally hard. If origin matters to you, buy natural G-VS2 — it is a real mined diamond with full GIA certification and the correct grade for this weight. The decision is about origin, not quality.

### Should I use platinum or white gold for a 1.5ct radiant ring?

Both work well — platinum is the better long-term choice, white gold is the better entry-price choice. Platinum never needs replating, develops a patina rather than wearing thin, and is the more durable metal for daily wear. At 1.5ct, where the ring investment is significant, the $340 platinum premium over 14K WG is usually worth it if budget allows.

### How many prongs does a 1.5ct radiant cut need?

Radiant cuts have four corners that must be protected with prongs — the corners are the most vulnerable point of the shape. The Ten Prong setting uses 10 prongs total, typically two per corner, which is the best protection available for a radiant cut. A 4-prong or 6-prong setting can also work, but adequate corner coverage matters more than the total prong count.

### Is a 1.5ct radiant cut ring good for everyday wear?

Yes — radiant cuts are among the most durable diamond shapes for daily wear. Unlike princess cuts, radiants do not have sharp corners prone to chipping; the brilliant faceting also masks minor surface scratches better than step cuts do. With a ten-prong setting securing all four corners, a 1.5ct radiant in any metal is a practical everyday ring.

### What is the best L/W ratio for a 1.5ct radiant cut?

Most buyers prefer a 1.20–1.35 length-to-width ratio — enough to read clearly rectangular without looking too elongated or too square. At 1.5ct, a 1.25 ratio gives approximate dimensions of 7.0 × 5.6mm, which reads as the classic radiant rectangle most buyers picture. Below 1.10, the stone looks square; above 1.40, it reads narrow and finger-lengthening rather than balanced.

### Does a 1.5ct radiant cut have a bow-tie effect?

No — radiant cuts do not produce a bow-tie. The bow-tie effect is caused by elongated brilliant facet patterns in oval, pear, and marquise cuts, where light escapes through the center creating a dark shadow.

Radiant cuts use a modified brilliant facet pattern across the entire stone, which distributes light more evenly and eliminates the bow-tie entirely. It is one of the practical advantages of the radiant cut over elongated shapes at this weight.

### How does a 1.5ct radiant compare to a 1.5ct oval in size?

A 1.5ct oval typically measures around 9.0 × 6.0mm — longer and narrower than a 1.5ct radiant at 7.0 × 5.8mm. The oval appears to cover more finger length because of its elongated shape, even though both stones are the same carat weight.

If finger-lengthening effect is your goal, the oval creates more of it. If you prefer a balanced rectangular look with no bow-tie risk, the radiant cut at 1.5ct is the stronger choice.

